<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Providence Island colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1630 CE – 1641 CE · capital New Westminster</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Puritan colony off the coast of Central America, a sister venture of New England that turned to privateering and slavery before Spain destroyed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providence-island-company">Providence Island Company</a>, whose investors included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-rich-2nd-earl-of-warwick">Earl of Warwick</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-pym">John Pym</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fiennes-1st-viscount-saye-and-sele">Lord Saye and Sele</a>, settled the small island of Santa Catalina in 1630. It was meant as a godly Puritan colony in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, planted with tobacco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and its leaders hoped it would outshine <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The company governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> through appointed governors and a council, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-westminster">New Westminster</a> as the main settlement. Crops failed to pay, so the colony turned to privateering against Spanish shipping and came to rely on enslaved Africans, who by 1641 outnumbered the English settlers. An enslaved revolt in 1638 was among the earliest in English America. Church life followed Puritan practice.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> saw the island as a pirate base on its treasure routes and attacked it, failing in 1635 and 1640 before capturing it in May 1641. The settlers were deported and the enslaved people seized. The company's leaders went on to play prominent parts on the Parliamentary side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/providence-island-colony.jpg" alt="Providence Island colony" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: Martocticvs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:English_Red_Ensign_1620.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1630 CE – 1641 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Westminster</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip Bell, Nathaniel Butler</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Puritan Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Providence Island, Old Providence</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=providence-island-colony" aria-label="Providence Island colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2449 1680 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Providence Island colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2769 1936L4970 663M2769 1936L4910 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2769 1936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2769 1936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1636&amp;focus=providence-island-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

